  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nThe proposed approach leverages a multi-resolution evolutionary strategy to escape local optima by combining global exploration with neighborhood refinement. This differs critically from the traditional phase-based optimization in the parent code by:\n- Employing a population-based search mechanism to explore diverse structural configurations\n- Incorporating adaptive mutation and crossover operations tailored to solution fitness\n- Implementing a dynamic fitness landscape analysis to guide the search towards more promising regions\n- Using a hybrid evaluation strategy that prioritizes computationally cheaper surrogate models during initial exploration\n\nThe primary bottleneck addressed is the risk of local optimum trapping in high-resolution grids. The approach betters the parent by systematically exploring solution space across multiple resolutions while maintaining a record of the finest-grained valid solutions.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n- Representation: Grid function h: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] represented as components h_i (n_points) to be optimized\n- Objective: Minimize C_5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n- Constraints: \n  - Integral constraint: \u2211h_i * dx = 1, where dx=2/n_points\n  - Box constraints: 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n\nThe optimization employs a multi-resolution framework:\n1. Coarse grid (n_base) evolution\n2. Fine grid (n_fine) optimization of base improvements\n3. Shell grid (n_shell) landscape exploration\n\nFor each step, we utilize:\n- Fitness: Decaying weighted C_5 to favor younger better solutions\n- Mutation: Adaptive gradient-based mutation with component-wise L1/L2 regularization\n- Crossover: Real-valued crossover guided by simplex geometry\n- Projection: Progressive normalization with iterative integral enforcement\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\nInitialize with parent resolution n0 = 400 if 'initial_h_values' is available\n\ndef run_evolution(resolution):\n    # Fitness function configuration\n    dx = 2.0 / resolution\n    target_integral = resolution / 2.0\n    \n    def fitness(individual):\n        # Enforce bounds and integral constraint\n        clipped = np.clip(individual, 0, 1)\n        integral = np.sum(clipped) * dx\n        # Integral adjustment using gradient projection\n        diff = target_integral - integral\n        free_indices = np.where((clipped > 1e-5) & (clipped < 1-1e-5))[0]\n        if len(free_indices) > 0:\n            normalized_weights = clipped[free_indices] / np.sum(clipped[free_indices])\n            clipped[free_indices] += diff * normalized_weights\n            clipped = np.clip(clipped, 0, 1)\n        # Compute cross-correlation\n        corr = np.correlate(clipped, 1.0-clipped, mode=\"full\")\n        return np.max(corr) * dx\n\n    # Parent search population\n    population_size = min(resolution, 50)  # Adaptive size\n    population = np.random.rand(population_size, resolution)\n    # Transform initial population\n    for i in range(population_size):\n        tmp = project_vector(population[i], target_integral)\n        population[i] = tmp\n\n    # Fitness calculation\n    current_best = None\n    for i in range(population_size):\n        f = fitness(population[i])\n        if current_best is None or f < current_best[1] or (f == current_best[1] and random.random() < 0.1):\n            current_best = (population[i], f)\n        if 'initial_h_values' in globals():\n            # Special handling for parent integration\n\n    # Adaptive spreading phase\n    for _ in range(generation_count):\n        # Dynamic resampling based on time budget\n        pass\n\n    return [current_best[0], current_best[1], len(current_best[0])]\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n   - Mutation rate: 0.1-0.3 depending on population diversity\n   - Population size: Scales from 20-100 based on resolution (min(50, resolution))\n   - Crossover probability: 0.7 with exponential scaling\n   - Fitness prioritization: Decaying memory with \u03c4=50-200 iterations\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n   - Time allocation:\n     * 40% baseline optimization\n     * 30% co-evolutionary checking\n     * 20% grid adaptation\n     * 10% resolution boosting\n   - Monitoring checkpoints required at:\n     * Generation milestones (\u00b130% buffer)\n     * Fitness plateau detection\n     * Performance degradation thresholds (\u226520% worse than previous best)\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\nVerification function:\n\ndef verify_solution(h, c5):\n    sum_val = sum(h)\n    if abs(sum_val * 2.0 / len(h) - 1) > 1e-5:\n        raise ConstraintError(\"Integral constraint violated\")\n    for x in h:\n        if x < 0 or x > 1:\n            raise ConstraintError(\"Value out of bounds\")\n    corr = np.correlate(h, 1.0-h, mode=\"full\")\n    new_c5 = float(np.max(corr)) * (2.0 / len(h))\n    if abs(new_c5 - c5) > 1e-5:\n        raise VerificationError(\"Objective calculation discrepancy\")\n    return new_c5\n\nFinal candidate formatting must ensure:\n   - Integral constraint met with tolerance 1e-5\n   - All values in [0,1]\n   - Optimization record preserved",
